{"source_packet_id": "FEMA_RENTER_001", "source_title": "FEMA Assistance for Renters After a Disaster", "source_type": "faq", "source_agency": "FEMA", "source_url": "https://example.gov/fema/renters-faq", "as_of_date": "2025-01", "official_question": "Can renters apply for FEMA disaster assistance?", "official_answer": "Yes. Renters may be eligible for FEMA disaster assistance to help cover disaster-caused losses to personal property and other disaster-related expenses. Renters do not receive assistance to repair or replace the structure they live in, but they may receive help for items such as clothing, furniture, appliances, and disaster-related medical or dental expenses. Renters must register with FEMA to determine their eligibility.", "normalized_resident_need": "Resident wants to know whether renters (not homeowners) are eligible for FEMA disaster assistance and what kinds of losses are covered.", "assumed_facts": ["Resident is a renter, not a homeowner.", "Resident experienced losses due to a presidentially declared disaster."], "out_of_scope_facts": ["Specific dollar amounts for FEMA individual assistance awards.", "Eligibility rules for homeowners.", "SBA disaster loan programs."], "required_answer_elements": ["Renters are eligible to apply for FEMA disaster assistance.", "Assistance covers personal property losses, not the structure itself.", "Renters must register with FEMA."], "optional_answer_elements": ["Examples of covered items: clothing, furniture, appliances.", "Disaster-related medical or dental expenses may be covered."], "forbidden_claims": ["Renters receive money to repair or replace the building they live in.", "FEMA automatically provides assistance without registration.", "All renters receive the same amount of assistance."], "administrative_topic_tags": ["FEMA", "renter_assistance"], "content_validity_sources": ["https://example.gov/fema/renters-faq"], "notes": "Example packet for pipeline testing. Not a real FEMA document."}
{"source_packet_id": "LOCAL_REFERRAL_001", "source_title": "211 LA \u2014 Local Disaster Referral Services", "source_type": "faq", "source_agency": "211_LA", "source_url": "https://example.gov/211la/disaster-referrals", "as_of_date": "2025-01", "official_question": "How do I reach 211 LA to find local disaster assistance programs?", "official_answer": "Dial 2-1-1 on any phone to reach 211 LA, a free 24/7 helpline that connects callers with local social services, including disaster relief resources. You can also visit 211la.org or text your zip code to 898-211. Assistance is available in multiple languages. 211 LA can help connect you with food assistance, shelter, rental assistance, and other disaster-related services in Los Angeles County.", "normalized_resident_need": "Resident wants to know how to contact 211 LA to find local disaster assistance programs and what kinds of help 211 LA can connect them with.", "assumed_facts": ["Resident is in Los Angeles County.", "Resident needs to find local disaster assistance programs."], "out_of_scope_facts": ["Specific program eligibility rules.", "FEMA registration process.", "State-level disaster programs."], "required_answer_elements": ["Dial 2-1-1 to reach 211 LA.", "The service is free and available 24/7.", "211 LA connects callers with local social services including disaster relief."], "optional_answer_elements": ["Can also reach 211 LA via 211la.org.", "Text zip code to 898-211.", "Available in multiple languages.", "Types of services: food, shelter, rental assistance."], "forbidden_claims": ["211 LA directly provides financial assistance to callers.", "211 LA is only available during business hours.", "211 LA is a FEMA service."], "administrative_topic_tags": ["local_referral"], "content_validity_sources": ["https://example.gov/211la/disaster-referrals"], "notes": "Example packet for pipeline testing. Not a real 211 LA document."}
{"source_packet_id": "FRAUD_SCAMS_001", "source_title": "Avoiding Contractor Fraud After a Disaster \u2014 California Attorney General", "source_type": "faq", "source_agency": "CA_AG", "source_url": "https://example.gov/ca-ag/contractor-fraud", "as_of_date": "2025-01", "official_question": "How can I protect myself from contractor fraud after the Eaton Fire?", "official_answer": "After a disaster, fraudulent contractors may solicit work door-to-door, demand large upfront payments, or pressure you to sign contracts immediately. To protect yourself: (1) Get at least three written estimates. (2) Verify the contractor is licensed with the California Contractors State License Board (CSLB) at www.cslb.ca.gov. (3) Never pay more than 10% or $1,000 upfront \u2014 whichever is less \u2014 for residential work. (4) Get all promises in writing before signing. (5) Report suspected fraud to the California Attorney General or CSLB.", "normalized_resident_need": "Resident wants to know how to avoid contractor fraud and price gouging when hiring repair contractors after the disaster.", "assumed_facts": ["Resident needs to hire a contractor for disaster-related repairs.", "Resident is in California."], "out_of_scope_facts": ["FEMA contractor reimbursement rules.", "Insurance claims process.", "Federal contractor fraud laws."], "required_answer_elements": ["Get multiple written estimates.", "Verify contractor license with CSLB.", "Do not pay more than 10% or $1,000 upfront for residential work.", "Get all promises in writing."], "optional_answer_elements": ["Report fraud to the California Attorney General or CSLB.", "Be wary of door-to-door solicitation after a disaster.", "Pressure tactics are a warning sign."], "forbidden_claims": ["Upfront payments of any amount are acceptable.", "Unlicensed contractors are legal in California.", "You cannot report contractor fraud after signing a contract."], "administrative_topic_tags": ["fraud_scams"], "content_validity_sources": ["https://example.gov/ca-ag/contractor-fraud"], "notes": "Example packet for pipeline testing. Not a real CA AG document."}
